"Study Details | NCT05039099 | A Study to Evaluate, Safety, Tolerability, and PK of AP-101 in Participants With ALS | ClinicalTrials.gov (https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05039099)\nciteturn198search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; Sponsor AL-S Pharma ... Last Update Posted 2026-09-30 ... This Phase 2a, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study evaluated the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and pharmacodynamic markers of AP-101 in adults with sporadic ALS or SOD1-associated ALS. ... Participants assigned to AP-101 received a 500 mg intravenous loading dose on Day 1, a 2500 mg intravenous dose on Day 2, and 2500 mg intravenously every 3 weeks beginning on Day 22 through Week 24.\n\n## A Study to Evaluate, Safety, Tolerability, and PK of AP-101 in Participants With ALS\n\nClinicalTrials.gov ID NCT05039099\n\nSponsor AL-S Pharma\n\nInformation provided by AL-S Pharma (Responsible Party)\n\nLast Update Posted 2026-09-30\n\nCompleted \n\nNo longer looking for participants\n\n## Study Overview\n\nBrief Summary\n\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the safety, tolerability, PK, and PD of AP-101 in participants with fALS and sALS.\n\nDetailed Description\n\nThis Phase 2a, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study evaluated the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and pharmacodynamic markers of AP-101 in adults with sporadic ALS or SOD1-associated ALS. A total of 73 participants were randomized within each ALS cohort in a 2:1 ratio to receive AP-101 or placebo during a 24-week double-blind treatment period.\n\nParticipants assigned to AP-101 received a 500 mg intravenous loading dose on Day 1, a 2500 mg intravenous dose on Day 2, and 2500 mg intravenously every 3 weeks beginning on Day 22 through Week 24. Participants assigned to placebo received matching intravenous infusions on the same schedule. Stable use of locally approved ALS treatments was permitted.\n\nParticipants who completed the double-blind period could enter an optional 24-week open-label extension in which all participants received AP-101. To maintain the integrity of the double-blind period, the initial dose in the open-label extension remained blinded. Participants were followed for safety and pharmacokinetic assessments for up to 16 weeks after their final study dose.\n\nThe primary assessments evaluated adverse events, serious adverse events, immunogenicity, vital signs, clinical laboratory assessments, physical and neurological examinations, electrocardiograms, and changes in weight. Secondary assessments included serum pharmacokinetics, AP-101 concentrations in cerebrospinal fluid, and the neurofilament biomarkers phosphorylated neurofilament heavy chain (pNfH) and neurofilament light chain (NfL). Exploratory assessments included the ALS Functional Rating Scale-Revised, slow vital capacity, muscle strength, King's staging, survival and ventilation outcomes, quality of life, and cerebrospinal fluid total and misfolded SOD1.\n\nOfficial Title\n\nA Phase 2a, Multicenter, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Study of the Safety, Tolerability, Pharmacodynamics, and Pharmacokinetics of AP-101 in Participants With Sporadic or SOD1-Associated ALS\n\nConditions\n\nAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis\n\nAmyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis\n\nIntervention/Treatment\n\n  * Drug: AP-101\n  * Drug: Placebo\n\nOther Study ID Numbers\n\n  * AP101-02\n  * 2020-005971-11 ( EudraCT Number )\n\nStudy Start (Actual)\n\n2021-09-02\n\nPrimary Completion (Actual)\n\n2025-08-13\n\nStudy Completion (Actual)\n\n2025-08-13\n\nEnrollment (Actual)\n\n73\n\nStudy Type\n\nInterventional\n\nPhase\n\nPhase 2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Study of VRG50635 in Participants With Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) | Clinical Research Trial Listing (https://www.centerwatch.com/clinical-trials/listings/NCT06215755/a-study-of-vrg50635-in-participants-with-amyotr